<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre-Paul de La Grandière</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1807">1807</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre-Paul de La Grandière was a French admiral and governor of Cochinchina from 1863 to 1868. He organised the administration of the three eastern provinces ceded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saigon-1862">Treaty of Saigon</a> in 1862, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> under French protection in 1863, and in 1867 seized the three western provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta, completing the colony of French Cochinchina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-paul-de-la-grandiere.jpg" alt="Pierre-Paul de La Grandière" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G.Garitan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_la_Grandi%C3%A8re_amiral_05097.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pierre-Paul de La Grandière was born in Redon on 28 June 1807 into a naval family from Anjou. His grandfather Charles Marie de La Grandière fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, and his father, Joseph Auguste Marie de La Grandière, emigrated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and returned to the navy under the Bourbon Restoration. He entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a> naval school in 1820, served as a lieutenant on the Trident at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Battle of Navarino</a> in 1827, and in 1837 took part in the French blockade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> under Admiral Louis François Jean Leblanc, distinguishing himself in the attack on Martín García Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Promoted to frigate captain in 1840 and ship captain in 1849, he commanded the corvette Eurydice in the Crimean War and took part in the unsuccessful Kamchatka expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He was rumoured to have shown cowardice by not bringing the Eurydice to help the Forte against Russian shore batteries, a charge he rejected. He later commanded the Breslaw in the Adriatic, headed the Syrian Station from October 1860 and became rear admiral in December 1861.</p><p>In May 1863 Prosper de Chasseloup-Laubat, Minister of Marine and Colonies, named him governor and commander of the Cochinchina naval station. He developed the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> with a refit basin and a floating dock, at first banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> exports to secure the local food supply, created schools, recruited Indochinese troops and abolished corporal punishment. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> expedition of Ernest Doudart de Lagrée and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-garnier">Francis Garnier</a> in 1866–1868 and supervised Rear Admiral Pierre-Gustave Roze's campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1866. When his forces occupied Vĩnh Long, Sa Đéc, Châu Đốc and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a> on 20–24 June 1867, their governor Phan Thanh Giản told the mandarins to submit without bloodshed and then killed himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>La Grandière began collecting artefacts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> and Chàm sites for display in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, laid the cornerstone of a new Governor's Palace on 23 March 1868, and ordered the collection of dossiers that became the origin of the French archives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in April 1868, became Maritime Prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> in 1870, retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finistere">Finistère</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a> on 25 August 1876.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pierre-Paul de La Grandière” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Saigon in 1862 Emperor Tu Duc of the Nguyen dynasty ceded three eastern provinces, and in 1867 Admiral <b>de La Grandiere</b> seized the three western ones, completing the colony of Cochinchina.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cochinchina">French Cochinchina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Redon, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.65,-2.09&amp;year=1851" aria-label="Redon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pierre-Paul de La Grandière"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4880 6046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4880 6046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.65,-2.09&amp;year=1851">What was here in 1851? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
